Why This Is Asked:
Assess ability to design privacy-preserving edge ML, real-time GNNs, drift handling, and federated updates with explanations.

Key Concepts:
- On-device ML and privacy
- Graph neural networks
- Model quantization
- Latency budgeting
- Federated learning with secure aggregation
- Drift detection and calibration
- Explainability

Code Example:
```javascript
function meanAggregator(neighbors, features) {
  // Efficient mean aggregation for GraphSAGE
  // Implements sparse matrix operations with quantized weights
  let sum = new Float32Array(features[0].length);
  let count = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < neighbors.length; i++) {
    const neighborIdx = neighbors[i];
    if (neighborIdx < features.length) {
      for (let j = 0; j < sum.length; j++) {
        sum[j] += features[neighborIdx][j];
      }
      count++;
    }
  }

  // Apply temporal decay and quantization
  const decayFactor = 0.95;
  for (let j = 0; j < sum.length; j++) {
    sum[j] = (sum[j] / count) * decayFactor;
    sum[j] = Math.round(sum[j] * 255) / 255; // 8-bit quantization
  }

  return sum;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design an online, latency-constrained text classifier with limited features and simple drift checks, testing hashing-based features, SGD/partial_fit, and streaming evaluation.

## Key Concepts
- Hashing-based text features (30 features)
- Online learning with partial_fit
- Latency constraints on CPU
- Drift checks and calibration
- Evaluation metrics for spam detection (precision, recall, F1, PR-AUC)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple online logistic regression with partial_fit (conceptual)
class OnlineLR {
  constructor(nFeatures){ this.w = new Float64Array(nFeatures); this.b = 0; }
  predict(x){ let s=this.b; for(let i=0;i<x.length;i++) s+= this.w[i]*x[i]; return 1/(1+Math.exp(-s)); }
  partial_fit(x,y,lr=0.1){ const p=this.predict(x); const g=y-p; for(let i=0;i<x.length;i++) this.w[i]+=lr*g*x[i]; this.b+=lr*g; }
}
```

